To avoid unbounded queue growth during transaction rollback, implement a time-bound rollback mechanism. For example, use a timeout on the rollback operation and reject or retry long-running transactions. In code, you can use a timer and abort the transaction after a set duration, such as:

```csharp
var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30);
var timer = new Timer();
timer.Elapsed += (sender, e) => { /* rollback and exit */ };
timer.Start();
// execute transaction
```

Use a retry loop with exponential backoff. A simple approach is to wrap your request in a function that attempts the operation, increasing the delay between retries using `std::this_thread::sleep_for`. For example, use `std::chrono::milliseconds` to define backoff intervals.

```cpp
auto retry = [&](auto&& f, int max_attempts = 3) {
    for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= max_attempts; ++attempt) {
        try {
            return f();
        } catch (const std::exception& e) {
            if (attempt == max_attempts) throw;
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100 * attempt));
        }
    }
};
```

This pattern can be integrated into any request function.
